# {{.Name}}

A web app built with Loom: a live-first Go framework with HEEx-like
`.hx` templates compiled to Go, and live pages rendered on the server
and patched in the browser over SSE with small diffs.

Loom is new and you have no training data for it. Don't guess: read the
docs (below) and let `loom check` tell you what's wrong.

## Commands

```
loom dev                      run ./cmd/{{.Command}} at http://localhost:4000, rebuilding and
                              reloading on every change (and migrating, with $DATABASE_URL)
loom check [--json]           parse and type-check every .hx template
loom build [--json]           check, then write the generated *_hx.go files
loom test [--json]            check, build, then go test ./...
loom status --wait            after saving under loom dev: did it rebuild, or why not
loom db new NAME              create migrations/NNNN_NAME.sql (needs $DATABASE_URL to apply)
loom migrate [status]         apply pending migrations, or list them
loom db install github.com/moomerman/loom   add the migrations of Loom's own tables after an upgrade (RT433)
loom components [--json]      list the starter kit (button, input, dialog, table, ...)
loom components add NAME      copy a kit component into components/
loom routes|sessions|inspect SID|render COMPONENT|errors --json
                              ask the app running under loom dev about itself
loom event SID EVENT KEY=VALUE
                              send an event to an open page, as a click or submit would
loom docs [TOPIC]             read Loom's docs
loom mcp                      all of the above as MCP tools (see .mcp.json)
```

Every command that prints something also takes `--json`
(`loom docs cli`).

## The rule

**Run `loom check` (or `loom build`) before `go test` or `go build`.**
A stale `*_hx.go` from an older template still compiles, so Go alone
won't tell you a template is broken or out of date. `loom build` both
checks and regenerates; `loom test` does all three.

After using a page, run `loom errors`: it shows server errors and, under
`loom dev`, the browser's script errors too. `loom events` shows what
the app did (handlers, commits, jobs), and `loom trace ID` one click's
whole story. `loom stats` counts events by route and job type, and
`loom queries` every SQL statement, with rates and timings: read both
after a change that touches a watched route, a job or a query. When
something is off, start with `loom doctor`. In an event handler,
report a failed write with `c.Fail(err)` and tell the user in the
assigns; never drop an error.

Every error has a code, a file:line:col with an excerpt, and a fix.
Apply the fix; `loom docs errors` explains each code.

## Where things live

```
cmd/{{.Command}}/main.go
                     starts the app: loom.New, Setup, app.Run
app.go               Setup: the routes (app.Live, groups, layouts)
counter.go           a live page: its state, Mount, Assigns, Events
counter.hx           the template it renders (component Counter)
counter_hx.go        generated by loom build; never edit, do commit
layouts.hx           the root layout: the HTML document
tokens.toml          design tokens: every color, space, radius, font size
counter_test.go      a loomtest test of the page, no browser needed
migrations/          SQL migrations, once you add a database (loom db new)
components/          starter kit components, once you add one
```

## One way to do things

- A page is a Go type with `Mount`, `Assigns` and `Events` in `name.go`,
  next to the component it renders in `name.hx`. Route it in `Setup`.
- Templates use a small, pure expression language, not Go: compute
  values in Go and pass them in as assigns.
- Styles go in a component's `<style>` block, scoped to it, with values
  from `tokens.toml` via `token(group.name)`. Raw colors and sizes are
  errors.
- Use the starter kit (`loom components add`) before writing a button,
  input or dialog yourself.
- Data: SQL in migrations, `loom.Select`/`loom.Get` to read, `db.Tx` to
  write. Background work: `jobs.Enqueue` in the same transaction.
- Test pages with `loomtest.Mount` and `p.Click(...)`, `p.Submit(...)`.

## Reading the docs

`loom docs` lists the docs in reading order; `loom docs TOPIC` prints
one (the `docs` MCP tool does the same). Read `getting-started`,
`syntax` and `live` before your first change. Then, when you need them:
`layouts`, `components` (tokens, styles, the kit), `data` (the
database), `jobs`, `storage`, `subapps` (auth, middleware),
`errors` and `cli`. `example-jobsweb` walks through a complete app.
